# tools/check-doc-routes-mutations.json — 11 mutation(s), case command: bash tools/check-doc-routes-selftest.sh --case {case}
skill-path	routes#aSkillPathThatNoLongerResolvesIsReported	a documented skill path is resolved against the skills the tree carries, so a renamed or deleted skill fails the check instead of a run that needs it
route-states-its-cost	routed-read#route	a row that routes a lettered sub-section states what those sections cost, so a row naming sub-sections and pricing nothing is reported instead of passing as routed
guideline-body-reference	routed-read#numbers	a citation a guideline body writes is resolved beside the routing table's, so a split's parent number that stops being a heading fails the case when a body still cites it
section-cost-ruler	routed-read#bound	a section's cost is the byte count of its lines from the `## ` heading through the line before the next one, so a printed cost that is not that count fails the case
route-figure-is-measured	routed-read#figure	a figure a row writes after a lettered reference is that section's cost, so a row pricing a kilobyte of section at 0.1 kB is reported instead of passing as priced
route-range-is-the-span	routed-read#range	a range a row states is the cheapest and the costliest of the lettered sections it names, so a range the measurement does not give is reported instead of passing as a span
companion-anchor	layers#aCompanionAnchorThatNoSectionCarriesIsReported	a companion's `## §N` anchor is resolved against its base document, so an anchor no section carries is reported instead of the companion becoming a document of its own
rule-name-one-document	rules#aNameTwiceInOneDocumentIsReported	one rule name stated twice in one document is the duplicate the one-normative-home rule forbids, so the name is counted per section and the second statement is reported
rule-name-two-documents	rules#aNameTwoDocumentsStateIsReported	two documents stating one rule name is the duplicate the one-normative-home rule forbids, found by the token the two statements share instead of by a reader's memory
rule-reference-resolves	rules#aStaleRuleReferenceIsReported	a `see rule <File>.md §N "<name>"` reference is resolved against the rule names the section carries, so a renamed rule leaves its references reported with the names the section does carry
rule-with-no-name	rules#aRuleWithNoNameIsReported	a rule with no name is reported with its file and line instead of being skipped by the extraction, so the empty case cannot pass as a rule the tree does not count
